<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">High Kingship of Ireland</h1><p class="st-meta">500 CE – 1198 CE (approx.) · capital Tara</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The prestigious title of king of Tara and over-king of Ireland, contested for centuries by the Ui Neill and later by provincial dynasties such as Brian Boru's.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The kingship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-of-tara">Tara</a>, an ancient royal site in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-mide">Meath</a>, was claimed from the 6th century by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Ui Neill</a> dynasties, who presented it as an ancient kingship of all Ireland. Their northern and southern branches alternated the title for several centuries, though actual authority rarely reached beyond the northern half of the island.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The high king was an overlord rather than a ruler of a centralised state: he took hostages and tribute from provincial kings and held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feis-temro">Feis Temro</a> and fairs. Monastic chroniclers and law tracts built up the ideology of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-kingship">sacred kingship</a>. Real power depended on military success.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brian-boru">Brian Boru</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-munster">Munster</a> broke the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Ui Neill</a> monopoly around 1002, and later kings ruled 'with opposition'. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruaidri-ua-conchobair">Ruaidri Ua Conchobair</a>, the last high king, accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II</a>'s overlordship in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-windsor-1175">Treaty of Windsor</a> of 1175, after the Norman invasion and the creation of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-ireland">Lordship of Ireland</a>; he died in 1198.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/high-kingship-of-ireland.jpg" alt="High Kingship of Ireland" width="266" height="404"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brian_Boru,_King_of_Munster.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 CE – 1198 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tara</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mael Sechnaill mac Domnaill, Brian Boru, Ruaidri Ua Conchobair</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old and Middle Irish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>, <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ard Ri na hEireann, Kingship of Tara</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=849&amp;focus=high-kingship-of-ireland" aria-label="High Kingship of Ireland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4668 398 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where High Kingship of Ireland lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4988 654L4861 599"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4861 599h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4988 654h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4988 654h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tara. <a href="/atlas?year=849&amp;focus=high-kingship-of-ireland">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

